WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.180
Give E. coli glucose and lactose together, and it does not eat them both at once.

2
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.960
It uses up the glucose, pauses, and only then starts on the lactose.

3
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:15.820
How does one cell choose?

4
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.940
The answer is in the DNA.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.560
Three lac genes sit side by side, and two separate signals decide whether they are

6
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.560
read.

7
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:27.883
The three genes are called lacZ, lacY and lacA.

8
00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:33.703
One promoter starts all three, and RNA polymerase reads them into a single messenger

9
00:00:33.703 --> 00:00:37.323
RNA. Next to them is a fourth gene, lacI.

10
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:39.383
It makes the lac repressor.

11
00:00:40.243 --> 00:00:44.543
The repressor binds the operator, a short stretch of DNA at the start of

12
00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:49.183
transcription. Bound there, it keeps RNA polymerase from transcribing them.

13
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.553
Lactose enters the cell through a carrier called the permease.

14
00:00:54.213 --> 00:00:58.573
An enzyme, beta β-galactosidase, turns a little of it into allolactose.

15
00:00:59.513 --> 00:01:01.273
Lactose itself is not the signal.

16
00:01:02.373 --> 00:01:06.233
Allolactose is. It binds the repressor and changes its shape.

17
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:11.133
The repressor then holds the operator more weakly, so it comes off, and

18
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.133
transcription starts.

19
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:16.597
Lactose alone is not enough.

20
00:01:17.397 --> 00:01:19.197
The lac promoter is weak on its own.

21
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:22.757
Full transcription needs an activator called CAP.

22
00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:28.797
When CAP is bound to cyclic AMP, it attaches just upstream of the promoter.

23
00:01:29.777 --> 00:01:33.217
There it contacts RNA polymerase and helps it bind and start.

24
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:41.450
Now add glucose. In the classic account, cyclic AMP is higher when glucose is

25
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.770
scarce, so CAP is active.

26
00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.570
When glucose is plentiful, cyclic AMP is lower.

27
00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.170
Scientists debate how much this matters in living cells.

28
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.530
Glucose has a second effect.

29
00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:59.370
While glucose is taken up, a transport protein binds the permease and blocks lactose

30
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:04.450
entry. Less lactose in means less allolactose, so more repressor stays on.

31
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.940
Put the two inputs together.

32
00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.940
With no lactose, the repressor stays on, and transcription is very low.

33
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.640
That holds even when glucose is scarce.

34
00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.860
With lactose and glucose together, transcription is reduced.

35
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.880
Lactose present and glucose scarce gives high transcription.

36
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:24.860
So the rule is simple.

37
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.560
The genes switch on only when lactose is present and glucose is scarce.
